{"kind":"report","text":"Independent final implementation-plan publication review for request #78, fulfilling promise #79.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVED at exact source head e106f95af01a6c6014bdea243dcebdec56fb50c5. No material publication or gated-handoff gap remains. This approval covers all seven cited artifacts and publication of the conditional implementation plan. It does not certify crypto feasibility, report runtime implementation complete, adopt the architecture, or authorize deployment.\n\nReview basis and checks\n- Read the complete revised 755-line Plan 001, the original signed design report #50 and its readable copy, report #60's N1–N13 findings and relevant fact-sheet claims, all supplementary dispositions, and the remaining publication files. Reused the prior exact-head review where bytes were unchanged.\n- Verified HEAD is e106f95af01a6c6014bdea243dcebdec56fb50c5 on request/implementation-plan with an empty git status. The diff from architecture baseline 2554c582db730cd3d64490970a573eab1463672e contains exactly the seven requested publication paths. git diff --check passed.\n- Checked all 21 local Markdown file links from those paths against the candidate tree; none is broken. The original report #50 body remains the filed text, and its publication file is unchanged from the first plan candidate. The dated architecture is preserved.\n- This is a documentation review. The repository still has no runtime package/test suite; no runtime command, crypto experiment, mirror, container, cloud deployment or benchmark is claimed to have passed.\n\nPLAN-01: RESOLVED\nPlan 001 lines 443–498 make P5 an independently verifiable retained-byte transport proof: primary/sequencer disabled, harness cache empty, signed/encrypted history plus definition/recovery-artifact bytes checked against known identities and a complete signed prefix, with a no-primary network trace. It no longer requires the P6 client.\nLines 500–538 let pure/store P6 work follow P2/P3/P1, but require completed P5 before P6 integration/completion. P6 must decrypt the promised history with declared G2 material, load the retained definition, and reconstruct canonical state/outcomes using the independent path; fixture-byte equality cannot pass.\nThe execution diagram (197–208), command contracts (672–673), A5 (693), P9 composition (620–656), and plans/README.md agree. There is no reciprocal P5/P6 completion dependency and no loss of full-replay evidence.\n\nOriginal finding coverage retained\nDESIGN-01–03 remain mandatory P1/P6 gates for irreversible interpretation despite MLS convergence, authenticated recoverable history and crypto-state durability, and common treatment of invalid versus unavailable input. Prefix extension stability, offline members beyond retained epochs, all-device loss, paused last-good state, and refusal to hide withdrawals remain explicit.\nDESIGN-04–06 retain distinct public admission and encrypted membership authority, exact control boundaries and in-flight/removal/retry behavior, invitation/recipient/Welcome binding, persisted randomized bytes, canonical wire vectors, and independently retained sender/account/action proof closure.\nDESIGN-07–09 retain complete-prefix retrieval separate from EOSE/freshness, independently tested mirror reader policy and replication holes/readback, serialized signing and atomic journal/outbox/admission updates, recoverable alarm scheduling, stale-restore signing refusal, and bounded ingress/storage/queues/retries.\nDESIGN-10–11 retain immutable local definition closure, bounded integer JSONata/Lexicon execution, operational-failure pauses, trusted and deterministic fixed-point-to-Float32Array conversion, future-script honesty, evidence identity checks and actual Workers-runtime testing.\nDIRECTION-01 still requires two unrelated definitions created after host startup, unchanged host/build/deployment identities, generic human and typed-agent controls, and two-instance isolation. SQLite journal storage does not introduce a required database projection.\n\nSupplementary integration assessed independently\nN1: the adversary-by-asset table now includes content, definitions, identities, membership, instance existence, timing and sizes, with declared visibility and fixtures.\nN2: long-term history and members offline beyond retained epochs are gate inputs. No static-key shortcut or weakened future-epoch exclusion is adopted; archive/snapshot coverage and custody must be specified and proven.\nN3: the wire/proof/retry decision is gated before integration. Encoded sizes are tested at every selected wrapper layer; neither a universal 24 KiB action ceiling nor mandatory external blob infrastructure is asserted.\nN4: public admission and encrypted membership remain independently verified. Rejecting an unauthorized transport request is correctly distinct from appending and interpreting an ineffective action.\nN5: the relay surface is bounded; NIP-11 units, behavior-changing paths, NIP-70 publication/replication constraints, exact framing and every enabled mirror read/query/reconciliation path are explicit.\nN6–N7: canonical state stays integer JSON; trusted schema/capability-bounded adapters render labels/assets, with hostile-input tests and deterministic buffer-byte comparison. GPU-pixel identity is not required.\nN8: v0 fixes the definition. G4/P2 must select transport refusal versus deterministic inner mismatch, with no silent definition substitution; activation remains deferred.\nN9: an online owner, pending/retry states and declared history remain required. Device admission must use a supported pinned model, not unfinished branch-draft interop.\nN10: retained checkpoints and conflicting signed evidence cause a pause; merely configuring a mirror does not guarantee discovery of equivocation.\nN11: transactions, hibernation, retry exhaustion and stale restores are tested. Cold-wake latency, actual row writes and workload-specific costs are measurements, not universal platform promises.\nN12: future commands must reject absent/skipped evidence. Authenticated per-instance provisioning and the generic typed agent adapter are deliberate scoped choices; a separate CLI binary is not necessary for this comparison.\nN13: the actual pinned protocol/library/account-proof/transport/device surfaces must pass P1. Reactive Nostr plumbing may be replaced, but a crypto/history change needs reviewed scope; the time box cannot turn an unresolved gate into a pass.\n\nPrimary-source qualifications independently verified\n- NIP-01 permits additional paths when they influence relay behavior; instance isolation and exact-URL authentication still need tests. [Pinned NIP-01](https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/01.md)\n- NIP-11 distinguishes serialized message bytes from content characters. Advertising limitations does not prove retention. [Pinned NIP-11](https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/11.md)\n- NIP-70 requires author authentication for accepted protected publication; its text supplies neither an application membership read ACL nor a genesis-to-sequencer registry. The restriction affects third-party republishing. [Pinned NIP-70](https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/70.md)\n- NIP-44's padding algorithm uses stepped buckets. Pinned strfry defaults are 65,536 normalized event bytes and 131,072 WebSocket payload bytes, and its configuration documents restrictReadToInvolvedPubkey. These support the plan's qualified framing/read-policy tests; they do not establish a universal plaintext allowance or current per-instance ACL. [Pinned NIP-44](https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/c3fd9af17939316bf6d0d83a5759100f8b0a1bdb/44.md), [pinned strfry configuration](https://github.com/hoytech/strfry/blob/4cd3cf64850caf47dda46c2a2abbbf3525a64d10/strfry.conf)\n- Marmot revision 4a2bc65f8db5866cec3b2a127dedb37818eaf207 explicitly marks the multi-device feature a branch draft with unfinished interop bytes; the proposed External Commit does not yet create an adopted membership-authorization exception. [Pinned Marmot multi-device](https://github.com/marmot-protocol/marmot/blob/4a2bc65f8db5866cec3b2a127dedb37818eaf207/features/multi-device.md)\n\nExecution boundary\nThis is a self-contained conditional handoff for a separate implementation request: baseline/drift checks, allowed paths, narrow Atseq reuse, P0–P9 dependency order, named future scripts, required-case evidence, acceptance matrix, versioning and STOP conditions are present. N1–N5 feasibility risks are not claimed resolved by prose; P1 must produce reviewed decisions and runnable evidence before dependent integration. The remaining choices are bounded gates for crypto/order compatibility, authenticated history/recovery, membership/device authority, canonical wire and encoded limits, and actual primary/mirror behavior. If any gate fails, its affected work stops for a reviewed change.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:108f118dd0a03fea63f319f72a67453c8e990524","head":"e106f95af01a6c6014bdea243dcebdec56fb50c5","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:4d52a9de4f835509a7144891d2a2d445e948c5ee\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:77aeeeb3fa42aeb6babdf961f6397ba73fada68b#git:sha1:4d52a9de4f835509a7144891d2a2d445e948c5ee","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}